s without the changed test executing. Examples: the job aborted before pytest, the module was deselected or skipped, or collection errored under a different name.","group_criteria":null,"group_id":null,"id":"f60b8128f0ecab0f3","impact":0.55,"is_primary":null,"location":"Acceptance Criteria — [non-codebase] sweep (windows) AC; Testing section ('shows neither stdin case in its failures')","norm_id":"nc1b67b3141e252c4","priority":0.3438,"reason":"default-advisory","recalled":false,"scenarios":["The Windows sweep job fails at setup or install, so no tests run and the test is trivially absent from the failures.","A skip marker or platform guard keeps the test from running on Windows, and the AC still reads as satisfied."],"severity":null,"suggested_fix":"Reword the AC: 'A sweep (windows) job on main after the merge shows `test_run_git_encodes_str_stdin_as_utf8[subprocess-run] PASSED` and `test_run_git_encodes_str_stdin_as_utf8[process-group] PASSED`. Check: `gh run view --job <id> --log | grep -E \"test_run_git_encodes_str_stdin_as_utf8\\[(subprocess-run|process-group)\\] PASSED\"` returns 2 lines. Record the run id and job id in a ticket comment.' If the sweep does not run pytest with -v, assert on the junit/pytest report artifact instead.","tier":"LLM","validity":0.625,"verification":{"binary":{"absence_confirmed_in_context":"yes","asserted_capability_confirmed":"na","cited_reference_accurate":"yes","claims_absence":"yes","committed_work_relies_on_unbacked_claim":"na","current_state_satisfies_plan_goal":"na","evidence_entails_finding":"yes","impact_follows_necessarily":"no","is_verifiable":"yes","no_existing_mitigation":"no","no_viable_alternative_explanation":"no","path_reachable":"yes","prerequisite_attribution_valid":"na","respects_artifact_altitude":"yes","severity_claim_justified":"yes"},"severity_attributes":{"ac_unverifiable":"underspecified_oracle","blast_radius":"local","debt_impact":"low","divergent_implementation":"none","dod_uncertifiable":"underspecified_certification","internal_conflict":"none","irreversible_without_rationale":"none","likelihood":"low","prod_impact":"none","reversibility":"easy","silent_vs_self_revealing":"silent","undecomposed":"none","vague_directive":"none"}}},{"block_threshold":0.95,"blocking_enabled":false,"checklist_item":"- [ ] Reword the Windows AC to require that the job log shows both `test_run_git_encodes_str_stdin_as_utf8[subprocess-run]` and `[process-group]` as PASSED, citing the run id and job id.","cohort":["E3","E5","F4","G5","T6","necessity","project.failure-disposition-contract","project.net-mechanism","project.portability"],"criteria":["E3"],"decision":"advisory","decision_margin":-0.6062,"drop_reason":null,"evidence":["\"A `sweep (windows)` job on main after the merge does not list `test_run_git_encodes_str_stdin_as_utf8` among its failures.\""],"finding":"The Windows acceptance criterion is satisfied when `test_run_git_encodes_str_stdin_as_utf8` is merely absent from the job's failure list. That would also be true if the test were skipped, deselected, not collected, or if the job aborted before reaching it. It is not end-state-observable proof that both `[subprocess-run]` and `[process-group]` cases ran and passed on Windows.","group_criteria":null,"group_id":null,"id":"f5da59a0e6f34048b","impact":0.55,"is_primary":null,"location":"Acceptance Criteria — [non-codebase] sweep (windows) AC line","norm_id":"nc484bdf3d8a89766","priority":0.3438,"reason":"default-advisory","recalled":false,"scenarios":["The Windows sweep crashes during setup or collection. No failures list mentions the test, so the AC is ticked without the fix ever running on Windows."],"severity":null,"suggested_fix":"Change the AC to: \"A `sweep (windows)` job on main after the merge reports both `test_run_git_encodes_str_stdin_as_utf8[subprocess-run]` and `[process-group]` as PASSED (verified via `gh run view --job <id> --log`). Evidence: run id, job id and the PASSED log lines in a ticket comment.\"","tier":"LLM","validity":0.625,"verification":{"binary":{"absence_confirmed_in_context":"yes","asserted_capability_confirmed":"na","cited_reference_accurate":"yes","claims_absence":"yes","committed_work_relies_on_unbacked_claim":"na","current_state_satisfies_plan_goal":"na","evidence_entails_finding":"yes","impact_follows_necessarily":"no","is_verifiable":"yes","no_existing_mitigation":"no","no_viable_alternative_explanation":"no","path_reachable":"yes","prerequisite_attribution_valid":"na","respects_artifact_altitude":"yes","severity_claim_justified":"yes"},"severity_attributes":{"ac_unverifiable":"underspecified_oracle","blast_radius":"local","debt_impact":"low","divergent_implementation":"none","dod_uncertifiable":"underspecified_certification","internal_conflict":"none","irreversible_without_rationale":"none","likelihood":"low","prod_impact":"none","reversibility":"easy","silent_vs_self_revealing":"silent","undecomposed":"none","vague_directive":"none"}}},{"block_threshold":0.6,"blocking_enabled":true,"checklist_item":"- [ ] Record the Windows text-mode stdin newline translation as a known `run_git` contract: add a line to the `run_git` docstring contract note saying text-mode `input_data` containing `\\n` reaches git as `\\r\\n` on Windows (use `text=False` with bytes for exact bytes), and reword 'the test's expectation is wrong' to say the test was mixing two properties.","cohort":["G6"],"criteria":["G6"],"decision":"advisory","decision_margin":-0.6,"drop_reason":null,"evidence":["Plan: 'which is platform behaviour of text mode and not this test's concern'","gitutil.py:207-217 and 239-251: text-mode stdin with encoding='utf-8' and no newline control, so TextIOWrapper writes os.linesep on Windows","Only current text-mode str stdin caller: src/rebar/_snapshot/delta_tree.py:204 (checkout-index -z, NUL-delimited), so this is latent, not live","gitutil.py docstring contract note covers bytes-vs-str but not newline translation"],"finding":"The plan treats the LF to CRLF stdin translation as 'platform behaviour of text mode and not this test's concern' and calls the test's expectation 'wrong'. In fact the translation is real behaviour of `run_git`, the wrapper the store uses for git plumbing. On Windows only, any str `input_data` containing `\\n` reaches git as `\\r\\n`. The `run_git` docstring's text-mode contract note says nothing about this. Dropping the `\\n` removes the only test that exercised it, so a future newline-delimited text-mode caller (for example `hash-object`, `mktree` or `update-index --index-info` input) would silently produce Windows-only differences, with no doc warning and no test to catch it.","group_criteria":null,"group_id":null,"id":"f1ebefa0108373b9e","impact":0.0,"is_primary":null,"location":"Approach / Context: 'The test's expectation is wrong'; src/rebar/_store/gitutil.py run_git docstring (lines 185-189)","norm_id":"nee523639fa479c4c","priority":0.0,"reason":"default-advisory","recalled":false,"scenarios":["A later caller passes newline-delimited str stdin through run_git (text mode). Object ids or parsed input differ on Windows while POSIX CI stays green, and the advisory Windows sweep has no test that would catch it."],"severity":null,"suggested_fix":"In Scope, add `src/rebar/_store/gitutil.py` (docstring only): extend the `run_git` contract note with 'On Windows, text-mode stdin translates \\n to \\r\\n (TextIOWrapper newline=None); pass bytes with text=False when the exact bytes matter.' Keep the test change as planned.","tier":"LLM","validity":0.5556,"verification":{"binary":{"absence_confirmed_in_context":"yes","asserted_capability_confirmed":"yes","cited_reference_accurate":"yes","claims_absence":"yes","committed_work_relies_on_unbacked_claim":"na","current_state_satisfies_plan_goal":"na","evidence_entails_finding":"yes","impact_follows_necessarily":"no","is_verifiable":"yes","no_existing_mitigation":"no","no_viable_alternative_explanation":"no","path_reachable":"no","prerequisite_attribution_valid":"na","respects_artifact_altitude":"yes","severity_claim_justified":"yes"},"severity_attributes":{"ac_unverifiable":"none","blast_radius":"local","debt_impact":"low","divergent_implementation":"none","dod_uncertifiable":"none","internal_conflict":"none","irreversible_without_rationale":"none","likelihood":"low","prod_impact":"low","reversibility":"easy","silent_vs_self_revealing":"silent","undecomposed":"none","vague_directive":"none"}}},{"block_threshold":0.6,"blocking_enabled":true,"checklist_item":"- [ ] State in the plan whether run_git's Windows LF→CRLF stdin translation is acceptable for its production callers, and if not, open and link a follow-up ticket.","cohort":["COH","E1","E2","E6","F1","T2","T4","T5b","T5e","T9"],"criteria":["E2","COH"],"decision":"dropped","decision_margin":-0.6,"drop_reason":null,"evidence":["\"On Windows, text-mode `subprocess` stdin translates `\\n` into `\\r\\n` ... so git hashes `café ✓\\r\\n`\"","\"the newline translation, which is platform behaviour of text mode and not this test's concern. No product code changes\"","No statement that production run_git str-stdin callers either tolerate CRLF or never send newlines."],"finding":"The plan establishes that `run_git` with str stdin rewrites LF to CRLF on Windows. It then rules that 'not this test's concern' without stating whether this is intended product behaviour. Production callers that feed newline-delimited stdin to git could be silently corrupted on Windows, for example `hash-object --stdin`, `mktree`, `update-index --index-info`, or commit messages. Removing the newline from the only test that exposed this removes the signal without recording the decision.","group_criteria":null,"group_id":null,"id":"f6f79f760c4a5c2fd","impact":0.0,"is_primary":null,"location":"Context / Problem and Approach sections","norm_id":"n7b2ad28a497379f0","priority":0.0,"reason":"low-validity","recalled":false,"scenarios":["A Windows user runs a code path that pipes a multi-line str to git via run_git, and the stored object or message gains CRLF line endings.","Later work assumes run_git str stdin is byte-faithful because its test passes on Windows."],"severity":null,"suggested_fix":"Add a sentence to Approach. Either: 'run_git str-stdin callers (list them) send no newline-sensitive payload, so CRLF translation is benign.' Or: 'Filed <ticket> to make run_git stdin byte-faithful on Windows (e.g. encode str to UTF-8 bytes before writing, or open stdin with newline=\"\").'","tier":"LLM","validity":0.125,"verification":{"binary":{"absence_confirmed_in_context":"yes","asserted_capability_confirmed":"na","cited_reference_accurate":"yes","claims_absence":"yes","committed_work_relies_on_unbacked_claim":"na","current_state_satisfies_plan_goal":"na","evidence_entails_finding":"no","impact_follows_necessarily":"no","is_verifiable":"yes","no_existing_mitigation":"no","no_viable_alternative_explanation":"no","path_reachable":"no","prerequisite_attribution_valid":"na","respects_artifact_altitude":"no","severity_claim_justified":"no"},"severity_attributes":{"ac_unverifiable":"none","blast_radius":"local","debt_impact":"low","divergent_implementation":"none","dod_uncertifiable":"none","internal_conflict":"none","irreversible_without_rationale":"none","likelihood":"low","prod_impact":"low","reversibility":"easy","silent_vs_self_revealing":"silent","undecomposed":"none","vague_directive":"none"}}},{"block_threshold":0.95,"blocking_enabled":false,"checklist_item":"- [ ] Audit run_git call sites that pass str input_data and record in the plan whether any is newline-sensitive. If any is, either fix run_git to write stdin without newline translation (e.g. encode to bytes itself) or file a follow-up ticket instead of only removing the newline from the test.","cohort":["E3","E5","F4","G5","T6","necessity","project.failure-disposition-contract","project.net-mechanism","project.portability"],"criteria":["E3"],"decision":"dropped","decision_margin":-0.95,"drop_reason":null,"evidence":["\"The test's expectation is wrong; the encoding under test (UTF-8) is correct.\"","\"the newline translation, which is platform behaviour of text mode and not this test's concern\"","Absence: no enumeration or audit of run_git call sites that pass str input_data, and no statement that none of them is newline-sensitive."],"finding":"The plan concludes 'The test's expectation is wrong' and calls LF→CRLF translation 'platform behaviour of text mode and not this test's concern'. It never checks whether production callers of run_git that pass str input_data depend on byte-exact LF content. On Windows, every str stdin piped through run_git gets CRLF line endings. That would change content for callers piping blob content, patches, or commit messages. The failing test may have found a real Windows defect, and the fix would hide it rather than resolve it.","group_criteria":null,"group_id":null,"id":"f81415131f03c2154","impact":0.0,"is_primary":null,"location":"Context / Problem — root-cause paragraph; Approach","norm_id":"nc518fe5184ddbb00","priority":0.0,"reason":"low-validity","recalled":false,"scenarios":["A Windows user runs a rebar operation that pipes a patch or blob body through run_git as str. git receives CRLF content, so blob ids differ from other platforms, or `git apply` rejects or mangles the patch."],"severity":null,"suggested_fix":"","tier":"LLM","validity":0.2222,"verification":{"binary":{"absence_confirmed_in_context":"yes","asserted_capability_confirmed":"na","cited_reference_accurate":"yes","claims_absence":"yes","committed_work_relies_on_unbacked_claim":"yes","current_state_satisfies_plan_goal":"na","evidence_entails_finding":"no","impact_follows_necessarily":"no","is_verifiable":"yes","no_existing_mitigation":"no","no_viable_alternative_explanation":"no","path_reachable":"no","prerequisite_attribution_valid":"na","respects_artifact_altitude":"no","severity_claim_justified":"no"},"severity_attributes":{"ac_unverifiable":"none","blast_radius":"local","debt_imp